{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-325899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $16{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}=64$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/state.json" "file_sha256" "5292b9c37a5f7aeb99349d58f34defbe238b8d16bcf2408d3f4f1dbab24a4650"} {"question" "q-325899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2} & =64 \\\\ \\frac{16{x}^{2}}{64}-\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{64} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have $a=\\sqrt{4}=2$ and $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/state.json" "file_sha256" "5292b9c37a5f7aeb99349d58f34defbe238b8d16bcf2408d3f4f1dbab24a4650"} {"question" "q-325899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c35c5c96549a22aec3fa395d4dac4dd4019a49651a81abae6a45792d1ad3398a"} {"question" "q-325899" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/refere