tion-capture/14053984/state.json" "file_sha256" "5292b9c37a5f7aeb99349d58f34defbe238b8d16bcf2408d3f4f1dbab24a4650"} {"question" "q-325985" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/state.json" "file_sha256" "5292b9c37a5f7aeb99349d58f34defbe238b8d16bcf2408d3f4f1dbab24a4650"} {"question" "q-89335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/23610b57f6867c18f11b0b334bb4902ab92f4b111a2764aa35b5a3a1cec77e2f.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/state.json" "file_sha256" "5292b9c37a5f7aeb99349d58f34defbe238b8d16bcf2408d3f4f1dbab24a4650"} {"question" "q-89335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/d91eb69ad250ab786426c2b46d5404bdf8aa43d6c97c1d276be444b678be4ced.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the left branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the left branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Develope