"file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-7883.json" "file_sha256" "0da9bf1a4f83b743f6b86cec1cab470d48aa5c08d625720d8cd5213f8a68dc04"} {"question" "e-7883" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a = \\sqrt{49} = 7$ and $b =\\sqrt9= 3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=3\\tan{t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-7883.json" "file_sha256" "0da9bf1a4f83b743f6b86cec1cab470d48aa5c08d625720d8cd5213f8a68dc04"} {"question" "e-5378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the asymptotes of the hyperbola below are $y=\\pm\\dfrac{5}{7}x,$ find the parametric equations of the hyperbola.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/fd2618e961c15a9b0665e73e18544ab08b0d611b962bc3771558c61450cf49e5.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-5378.json" "file_sha256" "569308a8a9290738ee5fa7004414dc72579506fb4a091c30d33819d18b7e94a3"} {"question" "e-5378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a, 0).$ From the graph, we have that $(7,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=7.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\dfrac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm\\dfrac57x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ \\frac{b}{7} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=5\\tan{t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-5378.json" "file_sha256" "569308a8a9290738ee5fa7004414dc72579506fb4a091c30d33819d18b7e94a3"} {"question" "e-5379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola with the parametric equations $x = 6\\sec{t},\\, y = 4 \\sqrt{2}\\tan{t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-5379.json" "file_sha256" "4038793e4b5799511d446578f0d430accc70bcb98c755682d84498b9e7a1298a"} {"question" "e-5379" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(4\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-5379.json" "file_sha256" "4038793e4b5799511d446578f0d430accc70bcb98c755682d84498b9e7a1298a"} {"question" "e-7896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/6e12d114aedfd6c5c8ab5830e052fcc1dd3c937b825665a0490b42ca0c87972c.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-7896.json" "file_sha256" "18e3aae9462394c6b5d87518635e0f4ec20cfadcacb052feead57bd9c892e2ff"} {"question" "e-7896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad\nt \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad\nt \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/730325137cfca58eb17d65103e539d084f81d7ab810628261125c29dbb8a0284.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $\\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad\nt \\in \\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/example-7896.json" "file_sha256" "18e3aae9462394c6b5d87518635e0f4ec20cfadcacb052feead57bd9c892e2ff"}] "usage" {"e-7896" [] "e-5379" [] "e-7883" [] "e-5378" []} "basis" 1792 "retractions" [[17592186087580 :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\displaystyle\\frac{x^2}{49} - \\dfrac{y^2}{9} = 1$?"] [17592186087580 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\na = \\sqrt{49} = 7\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nb =\\sqrt9= 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=3\\tan{t}\n$$"] [17592186087491 :question/problem "Given that the asymptotes of the hyperbola below are $y=\\pm\\dfrac{5}{7}x$, find the parametric equations of the hyperbola.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/873/Source/Images/6dac6525092db5cbe84cc8ace803557c.png)"] [17592186087491 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a, 0)$. From the graph, we have that $(7,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=7$.\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\dfrac{b}{a}x\n$$\n\nIn our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm\\dfrac57x\n$$\n\nand so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{b}{a} &= \\frac{5}{7} \\\\\n\\frac{b}{7} &= \\frac{5}{7} \\\\\nb &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=5\\tan{t}\n$$"] [17592186087492 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola with the parametric equations $x = 6\\sec{t},\\, y = 4 \\sqrt{2}\\tan{t}$."] [17592186087492 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t$. We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 6\\sec t \\\\\n\\frac{x}{6} &= \\sec t \\\\\n(\\frac{x}{6})^{2} &= (\\sec t)^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{6^{2}} &= \\sec^{2} t \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{36} &= \\sec^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4\\sqrt{2}\\tan t \\\\\n\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}} &= \\tan t \\\\\n(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}})^{2} &= (\\tan t)^{2} \\\\\n(y^{2})/((4\\sqrt{2})^{2}) &= \\tan^{2} t \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{32} &= \\tan^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}\n$$\n\nand rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan^{2} t + 1 &= \\sec^{2} t \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{32} + 1 &= \\frac{x^{2}}{36} \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{36} - \\frac{y^{2}}{32} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087581 :question/problem "The quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/873/Source/Images/c60e96f8795a9be0304c10941f61c928.png)\n\n$x = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?"] [17592186087581 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad y = \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad t \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad t \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/873/Source/Images/446c464de9a66d886281a25f332bc654.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[MATH: \\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)]$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$[MATH: x= \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad y = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad t \\in \\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right).]$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
